PLACES=[
N('herman-melville-house-lansingburgh','Herman Melville House','Lansingburgh, Troy','troy','landmark','standing',42.77339,-73.67887,'exact','view','drive','','c. 1786; Melvilles 1838–1847',
  'A brick and timber-frame house of about 1786, remodelled in the late Victorian style about 1872, where Herman Melville lived with his widowed mother and family from 1838 to 1847. They moved here to save money and were close to poor; from here he made his first sea voyage, to Liverpool in the summer of 1839. The house was listed on the National Register in 1992.',
  'The house on First Avenue facing the Hudson, now the headquarters of the Lansingburgh Historical Society. The society says its two first-floor parlours hold furniture from local houses, a doll house and a box that held Melville and Gansevoort family letters, and the third floor has things from Lansingburgh\'s brick, brush and rope industries. Melville Park, across First Avenue, was once a shipbuilding site. Interior not checked beyond the society\'s description.',
  'The house where the young Melville lived before he went to sea.',
  'Lansingburgh Historical Society. Its home page gives no visiting hours; hours not checked, so ask through the society\'s contact page before going. Otherwise see it from First Avenue and 114th Street. Street parking (no public lot checked).',
  [W('Herman Melville House (Troy, New York)'),LHS('home page','')],brick='no'),
N('lansingburgh-academy','Lansingburgh Academy (Lansingburgh library)','Lansingburgh, Troy','troy','civic','standing',42.77315,-73.67570,'exact','open','drive','','1820',
  'The Lansingburgh Academy was chartered in 1796, under a charter signed by John Jay, and first met in a wooden building on the village green. This brick building on the corner of Fourth Avenue and 114th Street replaced it in about 1820. From 1900 it was leased and then sold to the school district as a high school; a branch library took a room in 1939, and after a restoration finished in 1980 the whole building became the Lansingburgh branch of the Troy Public Library, which bought it in 1999.',
  'The 1820 brick academy with a later addition at the back (built while it was a high school). It is open as a public library, so the inside can be seen during library hours; what original fabric survives inside is not checked.',
  '',
  "Troy Public Library, Lansingburgh Branch, 27 114th Street. Hours from the library's page (read November 19, 2026). The library says the branch has a small off-street lot.",
  [W('Lansingburgh Academy'),L('Troy Public Library: about and hours','https://www.thetroylibrary.org/about/')],brick='no',hours='Monday and Thursday 10 a.m. to 6 p.m., Tuesday and Wednesday noon to 8 p.m., Friday and Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Sunday and holidays'),
N('lansingburgh-village-burial-ground','Lansingburgh Village Burial Ground','Lansingburgh, Troy','troy','burial','standing',42.76468,-73.67944,'site','view','drive','cemeteries','1771–1912',
  'The old village burying ground of Lansingburgh, begun in 1771, soon after Abraham Lansing laid out the village. It holds about 350 graves up to 1912, and its sandstone, marble and granite stones run through the whole range of gravestone carving from the late 1700s through the 1800s. It was listed on the National Register in 2002.',
  'Sandstone, marble and granite stones along a middle path. The Lansingburgh Historical Society\'s page works from a DAR transcription in the Troy Public Library\'s scrapbooks and says many of the stones it lists can no longer be found, among them a Revolutionary soldier\'s plot whose iron rail is gone.',
  '',
  'On Fourth Avenue in Lansingburgh (OpenStreetMap). Owner and opening rules not checked: see it from the street unless a gate stands open. Street parking.',
  [W('Lansingburgh Village Burial Ground'),LHS('the oldest Lansingburgh cemetery','oldest-lansingburgh-cemetery')],brick='no'),
N('trinity-church-lansingburgh','Trinity Church Lansingburgh','Lansingburgh, Troy','troy','worship','standing',42.77478,-73.67507,'exact','open','drive','cemeteries','1869; parish 1804',
  'An Episcopal parish organized in 1804, whose first church, by Philip Hooker, was finished in 1805 and burned on Christmas Day 1868 when the Christmas greens caught fire. The present Gothic Revival stone church by Henry Dudley was begun in August 1869. The Register listing of 1995 takes in the church, a Greek Revival brick rectory of about 1844, a 1930 parish hall, a graveyard of 1807 with about 240 graves, and a wrought-iron fence of 1901.',
  'The stone church with a Meneely bell of 1877 in its tower, and the graveyard beside it, where the first rector, the Revolutionary War veteran David Butler, lies with other Revolutionary and Civil War veterans. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Trinity Church Lansingburgh (Episcopal Diocese of Albany), 115th Street and Fourth Avenue. Its page (read November 19, 2026) gives the service times and says ramps reach the church and hall. At other times see it from the street. Street parking.',
  [W('Trinity Church Lansingburgh'),L('Trinity Church Lansingburgh: about','https://www.trinitychurchlansingburgh.org/about-our-church'),L('Trinity Church Lansingburgh: home','https://www.trinitychurchlansingburgh.org/')],brick='no',hours='Sunday Holy Eucharist at 8:45 a.m.; Wednesday service at 10 a.m. in the parish hall'),
N('first-presbyterian-lansingburgh','First Presbyterian Church of Lansingburgh','Lansingburgh, Troy','troy','worship','standing',42.77439,-73.67553,'exact','view','drive','','1845; congregation 1792',
  'The congregation was organized in August 1792 and built a brick meeting house, 50 by 64 feet, at the north end of the village green, dedicated in June 1794. A new church was dedicated in April 1845, and a newspaper complained in 1848 that the rubble of the old one still lay on the green. The church was listed on the National Register (reference 100007954).',
  'The church at 570 Third Avenue, facing the old green. Which parts date from 1845 and what is inside are not checked: no description of the building was opened.',
  '',
  'Owner and present congregation not checked; see it from Third Avenue. Street parking.',
  [LHS('First Presbyterian Church','first-presbyterian-church')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('methodist-church-lansingburgh','Methodist Episcopal Church of Lansingburgh (Joy of Troy SDA)','Lansingburgh, Troy','troy','worship','standing',42.77573,-73.67528,'exact','view','drive','','1849; 1875, 1903',
  'A Greek Revival church of load-bearing brick, built in 1849 for Lansingburgh\'s Methodists on land bought from Anson Groesbeck, and enlarged and remodelled in 1875 and 1903. Its meeting-house form, with a front gable and a two-stage bell tower, kept to the Greek Revival when the Gothic was coming into fashion. Since 2007 it has been the Joy of Troy Seventh-day Adventist Church; it was listed on the National Register in 2016.',
  'Outside: the gabled front with pilasters, the two-stage bell tower and a herringbone brick areaway; the front loggia and doors are later. Inside, the Register form describes the upstairs worship room with a deeply coffered ceiling, curved oak slip pews and decorative stained glass, mostly from the 1875 and 1903 work.',
  '',
  'Joy of Troy Seventh-day Adventist Church, 600 Third Avenue. Service times not checked; see it from the street. Street parking.',
  [NR('16000412')],brick='?'),
N('lion-factory-lansingburgh','Lion Factory','Lansingburgh, Troy','troy','industry','standing',42.78149,-73.67391,'exact','view','drive','','1884',
  'A collar factory begun in 1884 for James K. P. Pine: four storeys of brick, about 53 by 250 feet at first, designed by M. F. Cummings for shirt, collar and cuff making and a laundry, with about 300 workers. In 1890 Pine merged five firms into the United Shirt and Collar Company, whose Lion brand was made here, and the plant grew until it filled a city block. Standard Manufacturing bought it in 1970 and kept making textiles in it; the state called it Lansingburgh\'s last textile mill when it was nominated to the Register in 2022.',
  'The block-long brick factory on Second Avenue (US 4), with a Pomeroy Foundation marker (2019). It was converted in 2024 into the 151-unit Lion Factory Apartments, so the inside is private.',
  'One of the biggest collar factories in the Collar City, now flats.',
  'Private apartments (Regan Development, 2024). See it from Second Avenue; the marker is at 750 Second Avenue. Street parking.',
  [L('HMdb: Lion Factory','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=313522'),LHS('Lion Factory','lion-factory'),L('NY Governor: 2022 Register nominations','https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/governor-hochul-announces-21-nominations-recommended-state-national-registers-historic-0'),L('Albany Business Review (2024), copy at Regan Development','https://regandevelopment.com/wp-content/uploads/Strong-demand-for-Lion-Factory-Apartments-in-Troy-Albany-Business-Review-002-1.pdf')],brick='?'),
N('old-house-lansingburgh','The "Old House", 580 First Avenue','Lansingburgh, Troy','troy','landmark','standing',42.77546,-73.67799,'exact','view','drive','','c. 1720',
  'Marked as "the old house" on Abraham Lansing\'s first plan of the village, this house of about 1720 is the oldest building known in Lansingburgh, according to the Lansingburgh Historical Society. It has a Dutch gambrel roof over one and a half storeys, older in form than the two-and-a-half-storey houses of the late 1700s. Lansing laid out his village grid and green round it in 1770 and 1771.',
  'A small gambrel-roofed house on First Avenue near the river. The date rests on the historical society\'s page alone. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owner not checked; treat it as a private house and see it only from First Avenue. Street parking.',
  [LHS('home page ("The Old House")',''),L('City of Troy: Central Lansingburgh Historic District leaflet','https://troyny.gov/DocumentCenter/View/375/What-is-the-Lansingburgh-Historic-District-PDF')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('st-pauls-episcopal-troy','St. Paul\'s Episcopal Church (Tiffany interior)','Troy','troy','worship','standing',42.73016,-73.69011,'exact','open','walk','','1827–1828; interior 1891–1893',
  'St. Paul\'s, incorporated in 1804, built this church in 1827–28 as a close copy of Ithiel Town\'s Trinity Church on the New Haven Green, but faced in blue-gray limestone from Amsterdam where Trinity used brown granite. In the early 1890s the galleries were causing structural trouble, and the rector Edgar Enos had the whole interior remade by the Tiffany Glass and Decorating Company, mostly under J. A. Holzer, between 1891 and 1893.',
  'Outside, the 1828 Gothic front is little changed. Inside is a complete Tiffany interior: stained glass by Tiffany and Holzer, glass mosaics, tile work, a jewelled glass altar rail, chandeliers, a filigree baptistery, stencilled ceiling, walls and organ pipes, and woodwork made locally to Tiffany designs. Wikipedia says only four Tiffany church interiors survive intact; the church calls it one of the only fully integrated Tiffany interiors in the world.',
  'A whole church interior by Tiffany, still in use.',
  "St. Paul's Episcopal Church, Third and State streets. Service and office times from its page (read November 19, 2026). It offers an online 3D tour. Downtown street parking.",
  [W("St. Paul's Episcopal Church (Troy, New York)"),L("St. Paul's Church: Tiffany at St. Paul's",'https://stpaulstroy.org/tiffany-at-st-pauls.html'),L("St. Paul's Church: home",'https://stpaulstroy.org/')],brick='?',hours='Sunday services at 8 and 10 a.m.; Wednesday Eucharist at 12:05 p.m. Parish office Tuesday to Friday, 9:30 a.m. to 1 p.m.'),
N('church-holy-cross-troy','Church of the Holy Cross','Troy','troy','worship','standing',42.73251,-73.68332,'exact','view','walk','','1844; chancel 1848',
  'An Episcopal church founded by Mary Warren, whose nave was built in 1844 to designs by Alexander Jackson Davis; Richard Upjohn\'s chancel was finished in 1848. In 1846 it began one of the first choirs in an American Episcopal church, singing the full English cathedral service, and the Mary Warren Free Institute was built beside it in 1863. The church closed in December 2009 and Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute bought it.',
  'The 1844 Gothic Revival church on Eighth Street, with the 1863 institute building to its south, at the foot of the RPI campus. Present use and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute owns it. See it from Eighth Street. Street parking.',
  [W('Church of the Holy Cross (Troy, New York)')],brick='?'),
N('cannon-building-troy','Cannon Building','Troy','troy','landmark','standing',42.73140,-73.69206,'exact','view','walk','','1835; mansard 1870',
  'The oldest building on Monument Square, designed by Alexander Jackson Davis with Ithiel Town and built in 1835: one of the few large Greek Revival commercial buildings left. It was four storeys of load-bearing brick until two fires after the Civil War; in the 1870 rebuilding a mansard roof was added on top. In the later 1800s it housed Frear\'s Troy Bazaar, then one of the city\'s leading stores.',
  'Five storeys, 22 bays along Broadway, with the 1870 mansard, bracketed cornice and pedimented dormers on the Greek Revival block. Wikipedia says the easternmost storefronts are original and that the upper floors are being made into an extended-stay hotel.',
  '',
  'Privately owned shops and offices at 1 Broadway; see it from Monument Square. Downtown street parking.',
  [W('Cannon Building (Troy, New York)')],brick='no'),
N('west-hall-rpi','West Hall (Old Troy Hospital)','Troy','troy','inst','standing',42.73171,-73.68311,'exact','view','walk','','1868–1869',
  'A French Second Empire building by Marcus F. Cummings, begun in June 1868 as Troy Hospital and run by the Sisters of Charity from 1869; it treated the city\'s poor and its industrial workers and added a nursing school in 1895. Empty from 1913 to 1923, it was sold to the Albany diocese and became Catholic Central High School, and Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute bought it in 1952. A campus story says cables tie it to the bedrock; an RPI engineering professor has said there are none.',
  'The mansard-roofed former hospital on the slope below the campus, now RPI\'s arts department, with a 1868 cornerstone plaque on the southwest corner. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute, a university building. See it from the street (Eighth Street and Sage Avenue). Street parking.',
  [W('West Hall (Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute)')],brick='no'),
N('winslow-laboratory-rpi','Winslow Chemical Laboratory','Troy','troy','inst','standing',42.73092,-73.68429,'exact','view','walk','','1866',
  'RPI\'s chemistry laboratory, built in 1866 in brick with stone trim and named for president John F. Winslow, who paid half its cost. Fires in 1884 and 1904 damaged it; it stopped being a laboratory in 1907 and became a shop, and by the early 1970s it was boarded up and marked for demolition. Listed on the Register in 1990, it held the Junior Museum from 2000 to 2004.',
  'The brick and stone laboratory on Eighth Street, now research labs of RPI\'s cognitive science and computer science departments. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute. See it from Eighth Street. Street parking.',
  [W('Winslow Chemical Laboratory')],brick='?'),
N('kate-mullany-house','Kate Mullany House (National Historic Site)','Troy','troy','landmark','standing',42.74008,-73.68136,'exact','ask','walk','','1869',
  'Home of Kate Mullany (1845–1906), the young Irish immigrant who in 1864 formed the Collar Laundry Union, among the first lasting women\'s unions in the country, and led it to higher pay. The house is part of a three-storey, six-apartment building of 1869. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1998 and a National Historic Site in 2004; it stays privately owned, run by the American Labor Studies Center with technical help from the National Park Service, and opened to the public in June 2023 after its restoration.',
  'The restored exterior, the third-floor Mullany apartment, the stairwell and the outside back stairs, and a yard with period plantings (National Trust page). Interior seen only by appointment.',
  'Where a laundry worker built one of America\'s first women\'s unions.',
  'American Labor Studies Center, 350 Eighth Street. Its page (read November 19, 2026) says the site is still being restored and has no regular visiting hours, and asks visitors to call (518) 331-4474; the National Trust lists it as Monday to Friday by appointment. Street parking.',
  [W('Kate Mullany House'),L('Kate Mullany NHS','https://www.katemullanynhs.org/'),L('National Trust: Distinctive Destinations','https://savingplaces.org/places/kate-mullany-national-historic-site')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('troy-public-library-main','Troy Public Library (Hart Memorial Building)','Troy','troy','civic','standing',42.72799,-73.69209,'exact','open','walk','','1897',
  'Troy\'s library goes back to 1799; this building, given by Mary E. Hart in memory of her husband William Howard Hart, was finished in 1897 to designs by Barney and Chapman of New York in the American Renaissance style. It is listed on the Register on its own and as part of the Central Troy Historic District.',
  'West and south walls of white Vermont marble, carved surrounds on the three windows facing Second Street, and inside a Tiffany glass window of the Venetian printer Aldus Manutius. Open as a public library.',
  '',
  'Troy Public Library, 100 Second Street. Hours from its page (read November 19, 2026). No library lot: street parking (two-hour limit north of Ferry Street), and the Rensselaer County Courthouse lot after 5 pm and at weekends.',
  [W('Troy Public Library'),L('Troy Public Library: about and hours','https://www.thetroylibrary.org/about/')],brick='?',hours='Monday to Wednesday 9 a.m. to 6 p.m., Thursday 10 a.m. to 7 p.m., Friday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Sunday and holidays'),
N('united-waste-fortress-troy','United Waste Manufacturing Company Building ("The Fortress")','South Troy','troy','industry','standing',42.71563,-73.69730,'exact','view','drive','','c. 1902',
  'A six-storey brick warehouse of about 1902, built like a castle: castellated Romanesque detail, three-storey blind arcades, crenellated corner towers and a main tower like a keep. It was built for the United Waste Manufacturing Company, which dealt in cotton and wool waste and made shoddy; its president John J. Ryan founded the Waste and Shoddy Exchange in 1902. Also called the Hudson River Terminal Warehouse, it was listed on the Register in 2013.',
  'The tower and arcades face the Hudson and can be seen for miles from the west bank, including from I-787. The 2013 Register form says the open floors and a spiral stair from basement to top survive inside and that it was in use for storage. Present use not checked.',
  'A shoddy dealer\'s castle on the river.',
  'Private. 1 Jackson Street, at its west end, South Troy; see it from the street or from across the river. Street parking.',
  [W('United Waste Manufacturing Company Building'),NR('13000054')],brick='?'),
N('washington-park-troy','Washington Park','Troy','troy','landmark','standing',42.72412,-73.69249,'site','view','walk','','1839',
  'In 1839 six Troy businessmen bought ground between Second and Third streets and laid out Washington Park on the model of the London squares of Bloomsbury, with rows of townhouses built to one unified front. The park is reserved for the residents, whose deeds still require a maintenance fee; it is one of only two privately owned urban parks in New York. A new row went up on the park later, and the area is part of the Central Troy Historic District.',
  'The fenced square with the townhouse rows round it, and the Uri Gilbert Mansion, the most important detached Italianate house of the period, on Second Street west of the park.',
  '',
  'The park itself is private, for residents only; walk round it on the public streets. Street parking.',
  [W('Central Troy Historic District')],brick='?'),
N('emma-willard-school-mount-ida','Emma Willard School (Mount Ida campus)','Troy','troy','inst','standing',42.71135,-73.66405,'site','view','drive','','1910 campus; school 1814',
  'Emma Willard founded the school in 1814 at Middlebury, Vermont; moved to Troy as the Troy Female Seminary, it was the first institution of higher education for women in the United States. A new campus was built for it on Mount Ida in 1910. It is still a girls\' day and boarding school.',
  'A 137-acre campus with about 30 buildings; the three oldest are collegiate Gothic and hold a reading room like a cathedral, the auditorium, residence halls and a chapel. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private school, 285 Pawling Avenue. See the buildings from Pawling Avenue only.',
  [W('Emma Willard School')],brick='?'),
]
